@page "/legal/privacy"
@model LabelsMis.Web.Pages.Legal.PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy policy";
    var op = Model.Operator;
}

<div class="row justify-content-center">
    <article class="col-lg-8" id="legal-privacy">
        <h1 class="h3">LabelShop privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="text-muted">Last updated November 11, 2026</p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">1. Who runs LabelShop</h2>
        <p>
            LabelShop is the order, production and invoicing system that @op.Name runs for its own business. It
            is used by the people who work there and is not offered to the public. This policy says what
            information LabelShop holds and what happens to it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">2. What LabelShop holds</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Staff accounts:</strong> name, email address, role, and a password stored in hashed form.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>Business records:</strong> customers and their contacts (names, email addresses, phone
                numbers, billing and shipping addresses), suppliers, estimates, sales orders, jobs, shipments,
                invoices and payments, and the artwork and documents attached to them.
            </li>
            <li><strong>History:</strong> who created or changed a record and when, and a record of the emails LabelShop has sent.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4" id="quickbooks">3. Information from QuickBooks Online</h2>
        <p>When LabelShop is connected to the QuickBooks Online company of @op.Name, it receives from Intuit:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>sign-in tokens for that company, which it stores encrypted and uses only for the purposes listed in the <a asp-page="/Legal/Terms" asp-fragment="quickbooks">terms of use</a>;</li>
            <li>the QuickBooks company's name, its company ID and the settings that affect invoices;</li>
            <li>the customers, products and services, classes, payment terms and tax codes needed to match LabelShop's records to QuickBooks;</li>
            <li>for each invoice LabelShop sends: the QuickBooks invoice ID and number, and the tax and total QuickBooks calculated.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            LabelShop keeps only what it needs to match its own records to QuickBooks: names, IDs, and the
            figures on its own invoices. It does not copy the rest of the QuickBooks company's books.
        </p>
        <p>
            LabelShop keeps a log of every call it makes to QuickBooks: what the call was for, when it was made,
            who started it and whether it worked, with QuickBooks' error message when it did not.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">4. How the information is used</h2>
        <p>
            Only to run the business of @op.Name: quoting, producing, shipping and invoicing orders, and sending
            the documents and emails that go with them. LabelShop carries no advertising and no analytics
            trackers. Information is not sold. Information from QuickBooks is used only for the invoicing work
            described above.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">5. Who it is shared with</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The people who work for @op.Name, each according to their role.</li>
            <li>Intuit, when LabelShop reads from or sends to the connected QuickBooks company.</li>
            <li>The services LabelShop runs on, which handle the information only to provide that service: DigitalOcean (hosting, database and file storage) and Mailgun (email delivery).</li>
            <li>Anyone the law requires it to be given to.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">6. Where it is kept and how it is protected</h2>
        <p>
            LabelShop is hosted in the United States. Every connection to it is encrypted (HTTPS). Getting in
            needs an individual account, and each role opens only its own areas. Passwords are stored hashed.
            The QuickBooks sign-in tokens and the key of LabelShop's Intuit app are encrypted with a key that is
            kept apart from the database.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">7. How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
            Business records are kept for as long as @op.Name needs them. When the QuickBooks connection is
            removed in LabelShop, the stored sign-in tokens are deleted at once. QuickBooks IDs and figures
            already saved against LabelShop's own records, such as the QuickBooks number of an invoice, stay
            with those records.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">8. Cookies and your browser</h2>
        <p>
            LabelShop sets a small number of cookies, all needed for it to work: one keeps you signed in, one
            protects forms from forgery, one remembers your time zone, and one remembers how you like the jobs
            list shown. A short-lived cookie is set while QuickBooks is being connected. The pages load their
            typeface from Google Fonts, so your browser contacts Google when a page opens.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">9. Your information</h2>
        <p>To ask what LabelShop holds about you, or to have it corrected or removed:</p>
        @if (op.Email is not null)
        {
            <p>
                Write to <a href="mailto:@op.Email">@op.Email</a>@(op.PostalAddress is null ? "." : $", or to {op.Name}, {op.PostalAddress}.")
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>Ask a LabelShop administrator@(op.PostalAddress is null ? "." : $", or write to {op.Name}, {op.PostalAddress}.")</p>
        }

        <h2 class="h5 mt-4">10. Changes</h2>
        <p>The date at the top shows when this policy last changed.</p>
    </article>
</div>
